title The rule that reached 95 of 98, and the door nobody measured
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, send each finished increment
think Cold roster before touching anything: 40 of 40 green, every row in construction/REDS.md reads CLOSED, and both remotes sit at HEAD. The line is clear, so the lap is constructive.
think Commit count 3363, so the rota reads row 3363 mod 5 = 3 -- Water, Venus, care and flow. Gall-and-Conway fused, the happy zone and the thin edge, and how we grow a language. The first shaped the round: an organization of one durable person and forgetful agents communicates through written artifacts alone, so a front page is not decoration, it is the channel.
think The running thread from `20260824.062207` named Mycelium the standing half of the Tier 1 crux -- 98 modules, 80 witnesses, and it said no front page. Measurement disagreed: mycelium/README.md has stood since `20260813` at 12,789 bytes, under the pin bound, and it is good. Measurement beats memory, so the lap became read what is actually there rather than build what was predicted.
think Read what shape its roster takes, since that is what REDS %184 was about one directory over. Mycelium answers the same problem a better way: rather than listing 98 modules it names 31 bases across nine families and publishes a SUFFIX TABLE -- _bron, _true, _knot, _fixture_gen, _bench -- so a companion is reachable by rule. That reaches 95 of 98 on its own, which is how the page stayed a map instead of a catalogue.
think Three modules stood outside both the map and its rule: parse_int.rye and tally_copy.rye, seam symlinks into ../tally/, and puddle_fleet_fixture_gen.rye, whose name promises a base module nobody wrote. Small next to Caravan's 37 of 110, and quieter, which is the whole finding.
think Wrote the guard to read the suffix rule FROM the README rather than reciting a copy in the scan. The rule belongs to the page, so the guard asks the page what the rule is; add a suffix row and its companions are honored on the next run, drop one and every module wearing it reads unreachable, loudly.
think Then ran the register scan on the page, which had never been on the DOOR roster. It read 46% negative sentences against a 20% ceiling -- the exact number that founded Gauge Style on `20260823`. That turned out to be the larger half of the lap.
obs The transferable rule, and it is why %185 is worth a row beside %184: a rule-shaped roster goes short more quietly than a list-shaped one. A short list looks short. A rule looks complete, because a reader sees the rule rather than the set it covers, and a module whose name wears no seated suffix is invisible to the map and the rule at once. Caravan's fault was visible to anyone who counted; this one was visible to nobody, including me until I wrote the awk.
obs The second lesson has a number on both sides. Caravan's README joined the DOOR roster one lap ago and reads 5%. This one had never been on it and read 46%. A page joins the meter on the lap it is written, or it is measured by nobody -- and the four documents the pin law already prints over bound are the same lantern REDS %182 named, one layer over.
obs The guard bit my own edit on the lap it entered. Naming the two Tally seam symlinks, I wrote "beside [`kumara.rye`](kumara.rye) above" and the scan read duplicate_entries=1, since two entries for one module may come to disagree. Dropped to a plain code span. A guard that catches its author the same hour is the cheapest possible proof it is reading something.
obs Case 15 of the control misread on first run -- I expected unused_suffix=1 over a pen holding one module and two suffix rows, and the honest answer was 2, since `cord` alone wears neither. Corrected the case rather than the scan: my expectation was wrong, the reading was right.
obs Derivation stays ONE suffix deep on purpose and it costs nothing, since cord_knot_bench.rye resolves through cord_knot.rye, which is itself a named base. A chain that had to be walked would let an unnamed middle link hide behind a named end -- control case 12 plants exactly that and watches it bite.
obs Six sentences turned affirmative-first with every claim held exactly: "No node writes a balance" became "A balance is read rather than written", "never *arbitrated* by an authority; it is *resolved* by the order" became resolution-first with "rather than" carrying the contrast. Two negatives stay, and they earn it -- the lawful no-op and named refusal a contest produces, and the custody line naming no real key, no funds, no network. Refusal is the subject in both.
obs The hot roster paid for itself on this lap. Cold read 40 of 40 green; after `git add` three guards answered red -- `readme_metrics` and `geode_libraries`, because two generated indexes count tracked files and the new witness had just become one, and `readme_reach`, because the REDS row I folded to `construction/archive/` carried the pin's `../tools/...` links one directory down. REDS %183 taught that three hours earlier about index shelves, and the lesson had reached no further than the room it was learned in. Booked %186.
obs Then the repair over-applied in the same shape %183's patch did: a regex rewriting every `](../` also caught the header's `](../REDS.md)`, which the archive destination had already written correctly. Fixing that, I checked the SECOND fold file before staging rather than after, and found the identical fault waiting in it. The order is the whole lesson -- a fold moves depth, and a blanket rewrite moves what was already right.
